<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajax the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Telamonian Ajax</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ajax the Great is a hero of Greek mythology, son of Telamon king of Salamis, who fights in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> as the mightiest Greek warrior after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>. After losing the contest for Achilles' armour to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> he took his own life, a story told in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>' tragedy Ajax. His half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teucer">Teucer</a> was said to have founded Salamis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ajax-the-great.jpg" alt="Ajax the Great" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antimenes Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antimenes_Painter_-_Black-figure_Amphora_with_Ajax_Carrying_the_Dead_Achilles_-_Walters_4817_-_Side_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In Greek myth Ajax is the son of Telamon, himself a son of Aeacus and grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and of Telamon's first wife Periboea. He is the elder half-brother of the archer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teucer">Teucer</a> and, through his uncle Peleus, a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> describes him as huge and the strongest of the Achaeans, trained by the centaur Chiron, and he carries a great shield of seven cowhides covered with bronze. His name is of uncertain origin. One folk etymology links it to a Greek verb meaning to lament, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> gave another, from the eagle that Zeus sent in answer to a prayer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. Athenians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> traced their descent from him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the Iliad Ajax is chiefly a defensive warrior who covers the retreat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and protects their ships, and he is not wounded in any of its battles. In Book 7 he is chosen by lot to duel with the Trojan Hector, and the fight lasts most of a day until heralds acting on the will of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> call a draw. The two exchange gifts, Ajax giving Hector a purple war-belt and Hector giving Ajax a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-studded sword. Later, as Hector attacks the ships, Ajax leaps from ship to ship with an enormous spear and holds off the Trojans almost single-handed, though Hector burns one vessel. He often fights beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teucer">Teucer</a>, who shoots from behind his shield.</p><p>In Book 9 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> sends Ajax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> and Phoenix to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>' tent, but Ajax cannot persuade Achilles to return to the fighting. After the death of Patroclus he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> fight off the Trojans and bring the body back, and after Achilles dies he recovers Achilles' body for burial while Odysseus holds the Trojans back.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the council awarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>' armour to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, Ajax killed himself with his sword. In the Little Iliad he first slaughters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> of the Greek army in a fit of rage. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophocles">Sophocles</a>' play Ajax, Athena clouds his mind so that he slaughters sheep, imagining them the Greek leaders, and he kills himself rather than live in shame, with the sword Hector gave him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> says only that he lost the dispute, and in the underworld his shade refuses to speak to Odysseus.</p><p>Ajax was the guardian hero of Salamis, where a festival, the Aianteia, honoured him, and, after Salamis came under Athenian control, he became an Attic hero with a statue at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and a tribe named Aiantis. Since 2001 Yannis Lolos has excavated a Mycenaean palace at Kanakia on Salamis, which he suggests was the home of Ajax's dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ajax the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek tradition named Teucer, brother of <b>Ajax</b>, as its founder.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-salamis">Kingdom of Salamis</a></li></ul></section>
